# Rule: SwiftUI Visual Components

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/text-input-and-output
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/images
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/controls-and-indicators
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/shapes
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/drawing-and-graphics
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view-styles

Scope

Applies to visual primitives and UI controls in SwiftUI:

- text output and text input
- images and SF Symbols
- controls and indicators
- shape and path drawing
- graphics effects and compositing
- style application and style propagation

Intent

Use native SwiftUI components and style APIs to produce accessible,
consistent, and maintainable UI across macOS contexts.

Core Principle

Choose semantic components first, then style them.
Do not build custom visuals when a native SwiftUI control or style
already expresses the intent.

Implementation Rules

- Use `Text`/`Label` for read-only textual presentation.
- Use `TextField`, `SecureField`, and `TextEditor` for text entry by purpose.
- Use `Image` for local assets/symbols, and `AsyncImage` for remote loading.
- Use built-in controls (`Button`, `Toggle`, `Picker`, `DatePicker`, `Slider`,
  `ProgressView`, `Gauge`) before custom substitutes.
- Use `Shape` and `Path` for vector drawing; use `Canvas` only when immediate-mode
  rendering is justified (large/dynamic drawing workloads).
- Prefer style modifiers (`buttonStyle`, `toggleStyle`, `pickerStyle`,
  `textFieldStyle`, `labelStyle`, `listStyle`, `tableStyle`) at meaningful container boundaries.
- Let style propagation work; avoid repeating identical style modifiers
  on every child when one parent modifier is sufficient.
- Prefer `foregroundStyle` and `ShapeStyle` over hard-coded colors where possible.
- Preserve text readability with explicit `lineLimit`, `truncationMode`,
  and dynamic type-aware sizing patterns.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8) {
    Label("Invoice", systemImage: "doc.text")
    Text(invoice.title).font(.headline)
    Text(invoice.clientName).foregroundStyle(.secondary)
}
```

```swift
AsyncImage(url: model.logoURL) { phase in
    switch phase {
    case .success(let image):
        image.resizable().scaledToFit()
    case .failure:
        Image(systemName: "photo").foregroundStyle(.secondary)
    case .empty:
        ProgressView()
    @unknown default:
        ProgressView()
    }
}
```

```swift
Button("Save", systemImage: "checkmark") { save() }
    .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)
```

Anti-Patterns

- Replacing `Button` with `onTapGesture` for primary actions.
- Using custom text-entry wrappers that bypass native text controls without need.
- Hard-coding visual tokens per leaf view instead of applying style at container scope.
- Using `GeometryReader`/manual layout math to emulate standard controls.
- Overusing graphics effects (`blur`, blend modes, shaders) in data-dense screens.
- Using `Canvas` for static UI where `Shape` or standard views are sufficient.

Performance Notes

- Prefer lazy loading and asynchronous image phases for remote assets.
- Avoid expensive graphics/compositing in list/table rows.
- Keep row visuals lightweight in high-volume collections.

Checklist

- Does each visual element use the correct semantic SwiftUI component?
- Are style modifiers applied at the right scope?
- Are text and symbol choices readable and consistent?
- Are async image loading and fallback states defined?
- Are custom graphics used only where built-ins cannot satisfy requirements?

Architectural Implications

- Feature-level visual language should be defined by component contracts, not ad hoc per-screen styling.
- Shared visual behavior (button treatment, label density, icon rendering) belongs in reusable style boundaries.
- High-density surfaces (lists/tables/inspectors) should prioritize semantic clarity and rendering efficiency over ornamental effects.

Advanced Notes

- Style modifiers propagate through hierarchy; use this to encode design intent once per container.
- Immediate-mode rendering (`Canvas`) is a specialized tool for high-frequency drawing scenarios, not a default visual primitive.
- Graphics/compositing effects change rendering cost characteristics; prefer deterministic and minimal effect stacks.

Integration With Architecture

- In `InvoicingApplication/App/Scenes/Workspace/`, keep shell visuals conservative and predictable.
- In feature modules, standardize row/chip/badge components before introducing one-off variants.
- In data-dense billing and compliance views, avoid expensive effects in scrolling cells.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- semantic built-in controls and text/image primitives
- style propagation at meaningful container boundaries
- explicit fallback states for async visual content
- lightweight visuals for collection-heavy UIs

Never allow:

- gesture-only replacement of standard actionable controls
- custom control reinvention without platform need
- heavy graphics pipelines in rows and repeatedly rendered components
- per-leaf style drift that breaks visual consistency
